<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>13</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!--
      Zero warnings, enforced. A warning that is allowed to survive is a warning
      nobody reads, and 0.3.4 shipped its faults underneath a wall of them.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      Without this the IDE rules in .editorconfig only ever fire inside an
      editor, so a style rule set to error still lets the build through and the
      gate proves nothing.
    -->
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!--
      IDE0005 (unused using) is the one style rule the compiler refuses to run
      without a documentation file — it fails the build telling you so, rather
      than skipping the rule quietly. CS1591 goes with it: this codebase
      explains itself in prose comments that say why, not in XML doc tags on
      every public member.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>

    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Whichever NoMercy.PluginSdk.Abstractions and NoMercy.PluginSdk.Mvc that
      scripts/fetch-abstractions last packed into _nupkgs/ — or, once a server
      release has published them, whatever nuget.org carries. The major is
      pinned and the minor floats, as the server's own plugin template does:
      the major is the ABI a server accepts or refuses a plugin by (12 refuses
      everything under 12), and the contract adds members in a minor, so a
      pinned minor would make this plugin re-pin on every release that changed
      nothing for it. A bare * would one day rebuild it against a 13 nothing
      here was written for, and the first sign would be a server refusing to
      load it.

      Before 22 September 2026 this floated entirely and the packages carried
      the server's own version. That replaced a half-and-half that could only
      ever break: on 30 August 2026 the server went to 0.1.479, a fixed number
      still said 0.1.478, that version no longer existed to resolve, and
      NU1603 under -warnaserror turned a release commit red.

      What a floating minor costs, said out loud: the same commit built on two
      days can resolve two different 12.x contracts, so a build is not
      reproducible from the commit alone. What it was compiled against is in
      that run's own log, which prints the version it packed.
    -->
    <NoMercyContractVersion>12.*</NoMercyContractVersion>

    <!--
      The version, in the one place every project shares it. PluginIdentity and
      plugin.json carry it too and a test holds all three together: three
      copies of one number is how a server comes to report a version it is not
      running.

      Left to the compiler this was 1.0.0 for a whole sprint's development,
      which matters at exactly one moment — a deploy onto a server that was not
      stopped fails and leaves the old build in place, and that looks exactly
      like a deploy that worked. Somebody checking the file's version to tell
      the two apart would have been reading a number that never changed.
    -->
    <Version>0.7.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
